# Secrets -- never commit
*.env
.env

# state.db lives in R2 (tools/dbsync.py), NOT in git. It was tracked as the
# cross-run dedup store before R2 existed, and both mechanisms then shipped at
# once: the workflows all said "state.db lives in R2, not git" while six of
# them still ran `git add state.db`.
#
# The cost of that was not theoretical. It is a 64 MB binary with no delta
# compression, so every mutating run appended a fresh 64 MB blob -- the pack
# reached 112 MiB against a repo whose actual source is a few hundred KB, and
# GitHub hard-limits at 5 GB.
#
# History still carries the old blobs; this only stops it growing. R2 is the
# single source of truth now, and dbsync's health check + state.db.prev is the
# rollback story.
state.db
state.db.prev

# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/
venv/

# Local Claude Code tool settings (not part of the project)
.claude/

# Fetched full-text PDFs -- NEVER commit these. The repo is public, and these
# are third-party copyrighted works; they live locally only (tools/fetch_pdfs.py).
pdfs/

# Generated search index -- rebuilt from the state.db vector cache on every
# deploy (tools/embed.py), so committing it would add ~18 MB of new git blobs
# per run for zero benefit.
#
# vec.json belongs here too, and its absence was a real bug: it is the MANIFEST
# for vec.bin (row i of the .bin is uids[i]). Tracking one and ignoring the
# other let a stale manifest claiming 11,507 papers ship against a 8,390-row
# payload, and retrieve() read past the end of the buffer -- scoring NaN and
# scrambling the ranking, with no error anywhere.
docs/vec.bin
docs/vec.json
docs/abs/
# The parsed full text of every paper GROBID has read -- ~70,000 characters
# each, 2,382 of them, most from journal publishers. This repository is PUBLIC,
# so committing it was publication rather than personal use, and docs/abs being
# ignored while the FULL TEXT was not is the wrong way round.
#
# It is not merely dropped: it lives in R2 beside state.db now, pushed by
# tools/dbsync.py ftpush and fetched by tools/prepare_deploy.py on every
# deploy. Untracking it WITHOUT that would silently kill Ask's passage
# retrieval, the full-text markers and every Implement button -- nothing
# rebuilds it during a deploy, so being committed was the only reason it ever
# reached the site.
docs/ft/
# same story: the paper graph and the map are derived from the vector cache
# (tools/graph.py, tools/map.py) and are rebuilt on deploy -- 4.4 MB of new
# blobs per run otherwise
docs/edges.bin
docs/map.json
# the graph database itself -- derived from embeddings, 33 MB, rebuilt in ~12s
state_graph.db

# Blind bake-off report -- embeds paper text and the repo is public; it is a
# workflow artifact, not a commit.
bakeoff-report.md

# rendered bake-off page (built from the artifact report)
bakeoff.html

# your own PDFs, parsed by tools/ingest_dropped.py -- never committed
pdfs/manual/
# ...and anywhere else. pdfs/ only covers the directory tools/fetch_pdfs.py
# writes to; a paper downloaded by hand lands in the repo root, where it shows
# up as untracked in a PUBLIC repo and is one `git add -A` from being
# published. principal_portfolios.pdf was sitting there.
*.pdf

